INT. TARA'S BEDROOM - MORNING

TARA, an eight-year-old child with anxiety, stands at her desk. She wears a bright lilac rain jacket, striped socks, and sturdy sneakers. Her two short pigtails bounce as she nods.

Tara checks her small blue backpack. She pulls the straps, testing their strength. Click. Click.

On the desk sits her scrapbook. A blank page is labeled: YELLOW-BELLIED SAPSUCKER.

Tara wants that bird photo. She wants the library summer reading grand prize: the Orion StarSeeker Telescope. She can almost feel the cool metal of the focus dial under her fingers.

Tara checks her checklist.

TARA
(to herself)
Water bottle. Notebook. Extra pencils. Emergency whistle. Three flashlights. Laminated park map. 

She checks off each item with a neat green checkmark.

In the center of her desk sits her totem: a wooden weather-house toy. It is a tiny chalet with two doors. Inside the left door is a tiny wooden woman in a yellow sun hat. Inside the right door is a tiny wooden man in a dark blue raincoat.

Right now, the little man in the raincoat is sliding out of his door. The little sun-hat woman is retreating inside.

Tara taps the wooden roof. The rain man stays out.

Tara looks out her bedroom window. The sky is bright blue. The sun shines on the sidewalk. Not a single cloud is in sight.

Tara's mom, MRS. PATEL (30s), enters the room holding a dry sweater.

MRS. PATEL
Tara, sweetie, you do not need the rain jacket today. The weather channel says zero percent chance of rain. It is going to be hot.

TARA
My weather house says the rain man is coming out, Mom. And my skin feels... tight. Like a balloon that is too full.

Mrs. Patel smiles gently. She places the sweater on the bed.

MRS. PATEL
I know your brain likes to look out for everything, Tara. That is just how your body works. But today is going to be a beautiful day for the camp hike. Try to enjoy the sunshine.

Tara touches the strap of her backpack. Her heart gives a small, familiar thump. Her anxiety condition is already whispering secrets to her, but she zips her pack shut anyway.

EXT. SCHOOL YARD - DAY

A yellow school bus idles near the curb. Kids run around the grassy lawn.

Tara walks carefully, keeping to the center of the sidewalk. She avoids the high curbs.

CHLOE (8) and MASON (8) run up to her. Chloe wears bright pink sunglasses. Mason has a baseball cap backward.

CHLOE
Tara! Are you ready to find that bird? I brought my binoculars!

MASON
Why are you wearing a rain jacket? You are going to melt like an ice cream cone!

TARA
(softly)
The air feels heavy. The humidity is rising. I think we need to be prepared.

MR. DAVIS (40s), the cheerful camp counselor, blows a silver whistle.

MR. DAVIS
Alright, explorers! Single file onto the bus! Destination: Oakridge State Park!

INT. BUS - DAY

The bus rumbles down the highway. Kids are singing. Mason is showing Chloe a trading card.

Tara sits by the window. She is not singing. She is watching the sky.

Far to the west, the bright blue sky is turning a very pale, hazy gray. It is almost invisible, but Tara sees it.

She feels the pressure change in her ears. She swallows to pop them.

Tara pulls her laminated park map from her backpack. She traces the trails with her finger. Her finger stops at a small square labeled: STONE SHELTER.

She memorizes the distance from the creek to the stone shelter. Two hundred yards. Up a slight incline.

EXT. OAKRIDGE STATE PARK - DAY

The bus parks in a gravel lot. The kids spill out, laughing and shouting.

The park is beautiful. Tall oak trees line a sparkling creek. But the wind is starting to pick up. It rustles the dry leaves on the ground.

Tara steps off the bus. She takes a deep breath. Her nose detects the sharp, clean scent of ozone.

She walks over to Mr. Davis. He is checking his phone.

TARA
Mr. Davis? The wind is blowing from the east now. It was blowing from the west this morning. That means a low-pressure system is pushing in.

MR. DAVIS
(smiling)
Tara, I appreciate your help, but my weather app still says clear skies. You do not need to worry. We have everything under control.

TARA
But my mom says I have generalized anxiety disorder, and it means I notice things before they happen. My body is telling me the weather is changing.

MR. DAVIS
It is good to be alert, Tara. But sometimes a breeze is just a breeze. Let's go find your bird!

They walk onto the dirt trail. Tara keeps her backpack tight against her shoulders.

EXT. FOREST TRAIL - LATER

The group hikes deeper into the woods. The creek babbles loudly to their left.

Tara scans the high branches of the oak trees. She holds her camera tight.

Suddenly, she stops.

The forest has gone completely quiet. The cicadas have stopped buzzing. The birds have stopped chirping.

Tara looks at a nearby maple tree. The leaves are curling upward, showing their pale, silver undersides.

TARA
(pointing)
Look at the leaves. They are flipping over. They do that to protect themselves when a big rain is coming.

Mason looks at the tree, then at the sky.

MASON
It is just windy, Tara. Come on, the creek is right ahead!

CHLOE
Tara, look! On that branch!

Chloe points to a thick pine tree near the water. A small bird with a yellow belly and a bright red cap drills into the bark.

It is the yellow-bellied sapsucker.

Tara raises her camera. Her hands are shaking slightly. She takes a breath, steadying her elbows against her ribs. 

Before she can press the shutter, a massive crack of thunder echoes through the valley.

The bird screeches and flies away into the deep brush.

The sky turns an eerie, dark gray-green. The temperature drops instantly. A cold gust of wind knocks Mason's baseball cap into the dirt.

MR. DAVIS
Whoa! That came out of nowhere! Okay, kids, let's head back to the bus! Fast!

EXT. FOREST TRAIL - CONTINUOUS

The wind whips the trees. Rain begins to fall in giant, heavy drops. It stings their skin.

The kids scream and run back down the narrow trail. Mr. Davis leads the way, waving his arms.

CRACK!

A massive, rotting oak branch splinters from above. It crashes directly onto the trail ahead of them, blocking the path with a wall of thick wood and tangled leaves.

The creek beside them is rising rapidly, turning brown and muddy.

MASON
We are trapped! We cannot get to the bus!

CHLOE
I am scared! The water is coming up!

The kids crowd together, shivering and crying. Mr. Davis looks at the fallen branch, then at the roaring creek. He looks panicked.

MR. DAVIS
I... I cannot clear this branch alone. And the creek is getting too high to cross!

Tara's heart is beating like a drum inside her chest. The alarm in her body is screaming. But her brain is not frozen. It is running calculations.

She knows exactly what to do. Her preparation is her power.

TARA
Mr. Davis! We cannot go back to the bus. But we do not have to! The stone trail shelter is only two hundred yards away. It is on high ground!

MR. DAVIS
Are you sure, Tara? I do not see it on my phone. I lost signal!

Tara pulls her laminated map from her rain jacket pocket. She shines her bright LED flashlight onto the map.

TARA
I mapped it this morning. Follow me! I know the way!

Tara takes the lead. She does not run. She walks with purpose, shining her flashlight onto the wet rocks and slippery mud of the side trail.

TARA
Watch your step! Avoid the mossy stones! Hold hands!

Chloe grabs Mason's hand. Mr. Davis helps the kids stay in a line, following the bright beam of Tara's flashlight.

The rain pours down in sheets, but Tara's lilac rain jacket keeps her dry. Her striped socks are soaked, but her sneakers grip the wet dirt.

EXT. STONE SHELTER - MOMENTS LATER

Through the gray sheets of rain, a sturdy stone building appears. It has a solid slate roof and thick stone walls. It sits safely on a high rocky ledge, far above the rising creek.

TARA
There it is! Go inside!

The kids scramble up the stone steps and tumble into the dry, safe interior of the shelter.

INT. STONE SHELTER - CONTINUOUS

The wind howls outside, spraying water against the stone entrance, but inside it is dry and safe.

The kids sit on the wooden benches, shivering.

Tara immediately unzips her backpack. She pulls out three emergency space blankets. She hands one to Chloe, one to Mason, and one to Mr. Davis.

TARA
Wrap these around your shoulders. They keep your body heat in.

She pulls out her two extra flashlights and sets them on a stone ledge, turning them on to flood the dark shelter with warm, safe light.

Mason wraps the shiny silver blanket around himself. He stops shivering.

MASON
Tara, you are like a superhero. How did you know to bring all this?

TARA
My brain always thinks about what could go wrong. Usually, people tell me to stop worrying. But today, my worry was ready.

Mr. Davis sits on a bench, looking exhausted but deeply grateful.

MR. DAVIS
Tara, I am so sorry I did not listen to you earlier. Your brain noticed everything the weather sensors missed. You saved us today.

Tara smiles. Her chest still feels a little tight, but she feels proud. Her anxiety condition is not a fault. It is her early-warning system.

A soft, rhythmic tapping sound comes from the rafters above them.

Tara shines her flashlight upward.

Sitting on a dry wooden beam in the peak of the stone roof is the yellow-bellied sapsucker. It is preening its wet feathers, completely safe from the storm.

Chloe gasps.

CHLOE
Tara! Look!

Tara carefully lifts her camera. She adjusts the lens. She takes a deep breath, holds it, and clicks the shutter.

The flash illuminates the beautiful bird. The photo is perfect. It is sharp, clear, and beautiful.

TARA
I got it.

EXT. SCHOOL YARD - LATER - DAY

The storm has passed. The sky is a brilliant, clean blue again. Puddles glisten on the sidewalk.

The school bus is back. Parents are hugging their children. Mrs. Patel rushes over and hugs Tara tightly.

MRS. PATEL
Oh, Tara! I heard about the storm. Are you okay?

MR. DAVIS
She was more than okay, Mrs. Patel. Tara guided the whole group to safety. She was the only one who was prepared.

Mrs. Patel looks at Tara, her eyes shining with pride.

MRS. PATEL
I always knew your radar was special, sweetie.

Tara hugs her backpack. She checks the straps. Click. Click.

INT. TARA'S BEDROOM - EVENING

Tara sits at her desk. 

On the wall above her desk hangs a blue ribbon. Next to it is her scrapbook page, now featuring the beautiful photo of the yellow-bellied sapsucker under the roof of the stone shelter.

On her desk sits her weather-house toy.

Tara taps the tiny wooden roof. 

The little wooden man in the raincoat has slid back inside his door. The little wooden woman in the yellow sun hat is standing outside, enjoying the dry evening air.

Tara's heart gives a small, familiar thump. She feels a tiny flutter of worry about her math test next week.

She smiles, opens her backpack, and begins to pack her pencils, her sharpener, and her lucky eraser.

Her anxiety is still there, quiet and watchful, ready for the next adventure.

THE END